@page "/"

<PageTitle>DbContext concurrency demo</PageTitle>

<h1>DbContext concurrency demo</h1>

<p class="lead">
    This sample reproduces what happens when several interactive components call the same scoped user service,
    and that service holds the same scoped EF Core <code>DbContext</code>. It also compares several safe ways to fix or
    avoid the problem.
</p>

<div class="row g-4">
    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Scoped DbContext repro</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    Several child components call <code>FindUserByIdAsync</code> from <code>OnInitializedAsync</code>
                    at the same time. Because they share one scoped service and one scoped <code>DbContext</code>,
                    EF Core can throw its "second operation was started" concurrency exception.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-danger" href="scoped-dbcontext-repro">Open repro page</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Factory comparison</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    The comparison page keeps the scoped service lifetime, but each lookup creates its own
                    <code>DbContext</code> through <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;TContext&gt;</code>, so the same group
                    of components can load concurrently without sharing one context instance.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="dbcontext-factory-comparison">Open comparison page</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Serialized shared context</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    This variant keeps one shared scoped service and one shared scoped <code>DbContext</code>, but the
                    service uses a gate so only one EF operation runs at a time. Safe, but queued.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-info" href="serialized-scoped-dbcontext">Open serialized page</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-lg-6">
        <div class="card h-100 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4 card-title">Component-owned scope</h2>
                <p class="card-text">
                    This variant keeps the service directly injecting <code>DbContext</code>, but each child component
                    owns its own DI scope, so the scoped service and scoped context stop being shared across siblings.
                </p>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="component-owned-scope">Open owned-scope page</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
